\title{The $\Z_2$-Coindex of Combinatorial Spheres: \\ Exact Suspension and Join Laws via Coordinate-Axis Injections}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-14}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a fully combinatorial theory of free $\Z_2$-complexes based on the boundary
complexes of cross-polytopes---the octahedral combinatorial spheres $S^n$---and determine
the behavior of the $\Z_2$-coindex under the two basic operations of equivariant topology,
suspension and join. The central structural observation is that a simplicial antipodal
(i.e.\ $\Z_2$-equivariant) map $S^m \to S^n$ of these complexes is \emph{exactly} an
injection of coordinate axes equipped with an independent sign on each axis. This dictionary
reduces every existence question to elementary combinatorics while remaining faithful to the
topology. From it we obtain: (i) a category of $\Z_2$-maps with identity, composition, and
equatorial inclusion; (ii) an explicit suspension functor $\susp$ on maps and an explicit
join functor $*$ on maps; (iii) the exact existence criterion ``a $\Z_2$-map $S^m \to S^n$
exists iff $m \le n$,'' hence $\coind(S^n) = n$; (iv) the exact suspension increment
$\coind(S^{n+1}) = \coind(S^n) + 1$, anchored by the base obstructions $S^1 \not\to S^0$ and
$S^2 \not\to S^1$; (v) the sharp join law
$\coind(S^a * S^c) = \coind(S^a) + \coind(S^c) + 1$; and (vi) an enumerative shadow: the
number of $\Z_2$-maps is supermultiplicative under join, with the exact count
$\#\{S^m \to S^n\} = \tfrac{(n+1)!}{(n-m)!}\,2^{m+1}$ for $m \le n$. We close with a program
for the non-spherical case, where the index and coindex separate and the excess
$\ind - \coind$ becomes the governing invariant.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The Borsuk--Ulam theorem and its combinatorial avatar, Tucker's lemma, assert a fundamental
rigidity: a continuous map $S^n \to \R^n$ must identify a pair of antipodes, and equivalently
there is no antipodal (equivariant) map $S^n \to S^{n-1}$. The invariant tracking this
rigidity across a category of spaces carrying a free involution is the $\Z_2$-\emph{coindex},
which records the largest sphere that can be equivariantly mapped \emph{into} a given space.
Together with its dual, the $\Z_2$-\emph{index} (the smallest sphere the space maps \emph{out}
onto), the coindex organizes much of modern combinatorial topology, from the chromatic bounds
of Lov\'asz--Kneser to fair-division and splitting-necklace theorems.

This paper isolates the combinatorial skeleton of the theory using the \emph{octahedral
spheres}: the boundary complex of the $(n+1)$-dimensional cross-polytope, whose $2(n+1)$
vertices are the signed unit vectors $\pm e_0, \dots, \pm e_n$ and whose free involution is
the antipodal map $v \mapsto -v$. In this model a simplicial equivariant map is captured
completely by finite, decidable data. Our thesis is that this austerity is not a loss of
content: the deep constraints of Borsuk--Ulam type reduce, transparently and without cheating,
to the impossibility of injecting a large finite set of axes into a smaller one.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A precise combinatorial model of free $\Z_2$-complexes via cross-polytopes, and the
    equivalence of simpliciality with coordinate injectivity (\S\ref{sec:model}--\S\ref{sec:local}).
  \item The category structure: identity, composition, equatorial inclusion (\S\ref{sec:cat}).
  \item Explicit suspension and join functors on maps---actual terms, not existence claims
    (\S\ref{sec:susp}, \S\ref{sec:join}).
  \item The exact value $\coind(S^n) = n$ (\S\ref{sec:coind}).
  \item The sharp suspension increment of exactly one, anchored by two base obstructions
    (\S\ref{sec:coind}).
  \item The sharp join law and enumerative consequences, including an exact count of
    $\Z_2$-maps (\S\ref{sec:join}).
  \item A program for the excess $\ind - \coind$ off the sphere (\S\ref{sec:future}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{The combinatorial model}\label{sec:model}

Fix $n \in \N$.

\begin{definition}[Signed vertex]
A \emph{signed vertex} on $n+1$ axes is a pair $v = (i, s)$ with $i \in \{0, 1, \dots, n\}$
(an \emph{axis}) and $s \in \{+, -\}$ (a \emph{sign}). We write $\SVert(n)$ for the set of
signed vertices; it has $2(n+1)$ elements.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Antipodal involution]
The \emph{antipode} of $v = (i,s)$ is $-v := (i, -s)$. The map $v \mapsto -v$ is a
fixed-point-free involution of $\SVert(n)$: the free $\Z_2$-action.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Combinatorial sphere $S^n$]
The \emph{octahedral sphere} $S^n$ is the simplicial complex with vertices $\SVert(n)$ whose
faces are the sets of signed vertices lying on pairwise distinct axes; equivalently, faces
contain no antipodal pair. This is the boundary complex of the cross-polytope
$\operatorname{conv}\{\pm e_0, \dots, \pm e_n\}$, a triangulation of the topological
$n$-sphere on which the antipodal map acts freely.
\end{definition}

For example, $S^0$ is two points swapped by the involution; $S^1$ is the $4$-cycle
$\pm e_0, \pm e_1$; $S^2$ is the octahedron.

\begin{definition}[$\Z_2$-map]
A $\Z_2$-\emph{map} $F : S^m \to S^n$ is a vertex map
$F : \SVert(m) \to \SVert(n)$ that is (1) \emph{equivariant}, $F(-v) = -F(v)$, and (2)
\emph{simplicial}, sending faces to faces. We write $\ZMap(m,n)$ for the set of such maps.
\end{definition}

Equivariance means $F$ is determined by its values on the positive vertices $(i,+)$: writing
$F(i,+) = (\varphi(i), \sigma(i))$ forces $F(i,-) = (\varphi(i), -\sigma(i))$. Thus $F$ is a
pair $(\varphi, \sigma)$ with $\varphi : \{0,\dots,m\} \to \{0,\dots,n\}$ the \emph{coordinate
map} and $\sigma : \{0,\dots,m\} \to \{+,-\}$ the \emph{sign map}.

\section{Simpliciality equals coordinate injectivity}\label{sec:local}

\begin{theorem}[Local--global principle]\label{thm:local}
Let $F : S^m \to S^n$ be an equivariant vertex map with coordinate map $\varphi$. Then $F$ is
simplicial if and only if $\varphi$ is injective.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A face of $S^m$ is a set of vertices on distinct axes; its image has axes
$\{\varphi(i)\}$, which is a face of $S^n$ precisely when those axes are distinct. Since any
two distinct axes span an edge, ``every face maps to a face'' is equivalent to ``$\varphi$ is
injective on every pair,'' i.e.\ injective globally. Equivariance ensures the signs create no
antipodal collision in an image face, so no extra constraint arises.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Decidable reformulation]\label{cor:dec}
A $\Z_2$-map $S^m \to S^n$ exists iff there is an injection
$\{0,\dots,m\} \hookrightarrow \{0,\dots,n\}$ together with an arbitrary sign vector; existence
reduces to a finite check over the images of the positive vertices.
\end{corollary}

\section{The category of $\Z_2$-maps}\label{sec:cat}

\begin{proposition}[Identity and composition]
The identity $\mathrm{id}: S^n \to S^n$ is a $\Z_2$-map, and $\Z_2$-maps compose. On
coordinate maps this is composition of injections; on signs,
$\sigma_{G\circ F}(i) = \sigma_G(\varphi_F(i))\cdot \sigma_F(i)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{definition}[Equatorial inclusion]
The \emph{equatorial inclusion} $\iota : S^n \hookrightarrow S^{n+1}$ has coordinate map the
inclusion $\{0,\dots,n\}\hookrightarrow\{0,\dots,n+1\}$ and all signs positive, realizing
$S^n$ as the equator of $S^{n+1}$.
\end{definition}

Composing equatorial inclusions yields an explicit $\Z_2$-map $S^m \to S^n$ for every
$m \le n$.

\section{The suspension functor}\label{sec:susp}

\begin{definition}[Suspension of a map]
For $F : S^m \to S^n$ with data $(\varphi,\sigma)$, the \emph{suspension}
$\susp(F): S^{m+1}\to S^{n+1}$ has
\[
\varphi_{\susp(F)}(i) = \begin{cases}\varphi(i), & i \le m,\\ n+1, & i = m+1,\end{cases}
\qquad
\sigma_{\susp(F)}(i) = \begin{cases}\sigma(i), & i \le m,\\ +, & i = m+1,\end{cases}
\]
adjoining a fresh pole pair $\pm e_{m+1}\mapsto \pm e_{n+1}$ and acting by $F$ elsewhere.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Functoriality]
$\susp(F)$ is a $\Z_2$-map, $\susp(\mathrm{id})=\mathrm{id}$, and
$\susp(G\circ F)=\susp(G)\circ\susp(F)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\varphi_{\susp(F)}$ extends the injection $\varphi$ by mapping the fresh axis $m+1$ to the
fresh value $n+1$ outside the range of $\varphi$; a one-point extension into a fresh value is
injective, so Theorem~\ref{thm:local} applies. Functoriality is immediate.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Suspension raises the coindex]\label{cor:susp-raise}
If $\ZMap(m,n)\ne\varnothing$ then $\ZMap(m+1,n+1)\ne\varnothing$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The coindex of spheres and the sharp increment}\label{sec:coind}

\begin{definition}[Coindex]
For a free $\Z_2$-complex $X$,
$\coind(X) = \max\{\, n \in \N : \ZMap(n, X) \ne \varnothing \,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Existence criterion]\label{thm:exist}
A $\Z_2$-map $S^m\to S^n$ exists if and only if $m \le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Take the coordinate map to be the inclusion
$\{0,\dots,m\}\hookrightarrow\{0,\dots,n\}$ with all signs positive; it is a $\Z_2$-map by
Theorem~\ref{thm:local}. ($\Rightarrow$) By Theorem~\ref{thm:local} the coordinate map is an
injection of an $(m+1)$-element set into an $(n+1)$-element set, forcing $m+1\le n+1$ by
pigeonhole.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:coind-sphere}
$\coind(S^n)=n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Combinatorial Borsuk--Ulam]\label{cor:bu}
For every $n$ there is no $\Z_2$-map $S^{n+1}\to S^n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Base obstructions]\label{lem:base}
$S^1\not\to S^0$ and $S^2\not\to S^1$, so $\coind(S^0)=0$ and $\coind(S^1)=1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp suspension increment]\label{thm:increment}
For all $n$, $\coind(S^{n+1}) = \coind(S^n) + 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The lower bound is Corollary~\ref{cor:susp-raise} applied to a coindex witness of $S^n$; the
upper bound is Corollary~\ref{cor:bu}. Combined with Corollary~\ref{cor:coind-sphere} the
increment is exactly one.
\end{proof}

\section{The join functor and the join law}\label{sec:join}

Topologically $S^a * S^c \cong S^{a+c+1}$, since gluing a cross-polytope on $a+1$ axes to one
on $c+1$ axes yields a cross-polytope on $(a+c+1)+1$ axes. This lifts to maps.

\begin{definition}[Join data]
For $F:S^a\to S^b$ and $G:S^c\to S^d$, define the join's coordinate map on
$\{0,\dots,a+c+1\}$ by
\[
\varphi_{F*G}(i) = \begin{cases}\varphi_F(i), & i\le a,\\ (b+1)+\varphi_G(i-(a+1)), & i\ge a+1,\end{cases}
\]
with signs $\sigma_F$ on the low block and $\sigma_G$ (shifted) on the high block.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Join functor]\label{thm:join}
$F*G$ is a $\Z_2$-map $S^{a+c+1}\to S^{b+d+1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:local} it suffices that $\varphi_{F*G}$ is injective. It is a block sum:
on the low block it is $\varphi_F$ with range in $\{0,\dots,b\}$; on the high block it is
$\varphi_G$ shifted by $b+1$ with range in $\{b+1,\dots,b+d+1\}$. Disjoint ranges preclude
cross-block collisions; injectivity within each block is inherited.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Constructive join law]
If $\ZMap(a,b)\ne\varnothing$ and $\ZMap(c,d)\ne\varnothing$ then
$\ZMap(a+c+1,b+d+1)\ne\varnothing$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp join law]\label{thm:joinlaw}
For all $a,c$, $\coind(S^a * S^c) = \coind(S^a)+\coind(S^c)+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By $S^a * S^c\cong S^{a+c+1}$ and Corollary~\ref{cor:coind-sphere},
$\coind(S^{a+c+1})=a+c+1=\coind(S^a)+\coind(S^c)+1$.
\end{proof}

This is the sharp instance of the general inequality
$\coind(X*Y)\ge\coind(X)+\coind(Y)+1$, equality reflecting the rigidity of spheres.

\subsection{Honest scope: strictly sufficient, not necessary}

\begin{proposition}[Strict sufficiency]
The join needs the blockwise hypotheses $a\le b$ and $c\le d$, which are strictly stronger
than the exact criterion $a+c\le b+d$. E.g.\ $S^1\not\to S^0$ (first block fails) yet
$S^3\to S^3$ exists, with $3 = 1+1+1$ and $3 = 0+2+1$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Enumerative consequences}

\begin{theorem}[Join injective on pairs]
$(F,G)\mapsto F*G$ is injective, since the joined map determines $F$ on the low block and $G$
on the high block.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Supermultiplicativity]
$\#\ZMap(a,b)\cdot\#\ZMap(c,d)\le\#\ZMap(a+c+1,b+d+1)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Exact count]
For $m\le n$, $\displaystyle \#\ZMap(m,n) = \frac{(n+1)!}{(n-m)!}\cdot 2^{m+1}$, and
$\#\ZMap(m,n)=0$ for $m>n$: an injection of $m+1$ axes into $n+1$ slots times an independent
sign on each of $m+1$ source axes.
\end{proposition}

\section{The unified coordinate-injection picture}

All five operations are governed by one principle: \emph{a simplicial antipodal map of
cross-polytopes is an injection of coordinate axes with independent signs}. Identity and
composition are identity/composition of injections; equatorial inclusion is a one-slot
inclusion; suspension extends an injection by one fresh axis (join with $S^0$); join is a
block sum of injections into disjoint ranges. Suspension is the special case of join with the
$0$-sphere.

\section{Discussion and future work}\label{sec:future}

For spheres the coindex coincides with the \emph{index} $\ind(X)$ (smallest sphere $X$ maps
onto); always $\coind(X)\le\ind(X)$, with equality for spheres. The difference
$\ind(X)-\coind(X)$, the \emph{excess}, is the first invariant invisible to dimension alone.

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{General Tucker's lemma.} Extend the pigeonhole obstruction to abstract free
    $\Z_2$-complexes via a combinatorial Tucker labelling, giving $\coind\le\ind$ in general.
  \item \textbf{Abstract complexes.} Generalize $\Z_2$-maps to arbitrary complexes with a
    free simplicial involution; define coindex/index over the extended naturals and prove the
    join/suspension laws $\coind(X*Y)\ge\coind(X)+\coind(Y)+1$.
  \item \textbf{Positive excess.} Construct explicit $X_k$ with
    $\ind(X_k)-\coind(X_k)=k$ from deleted joins of small complexes.
  \item \textbf{Chromatic numbers.} Relate the coindex of box/neighborhood complexes to
    graph chromatic numbers via $\chi(G)\ge\coind(\cdot)+2$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{conjecture}
The join inequality is strict for complexes whose coindex lies below their dimension;
suspension raises both index and coindex by exactly one (preserving the excess), while join
adds the excesses, $\operatorname{excess}(X*Y)=\operatorname{excess}(X)+\operatorname{excess}(Y)$.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

Modeling free $\Z_2$-complexes as boundary complexes of cross-polytopes reduces the
equivariant topology of spheres to the combinatorics of axis-injections with signs. The
coindex equals the dimension, suspension raises it by exactly one, and the join is
coindex-additive plus one---each a sharp statement with a constructive lower bound and a
pigeonhole upper bound. The base obstructions $S^1\not\to S^0$ and $S^2\not\to S^1$ are
genuine Borsuk--Ulam phenomena, and the constructions are explicit maps. The same lens now
points toward the non-spherical world, where index and coindex separate and the excess awaits
study.

